<div class="learn-box">
**What you will learn**

Use translated concept answers, distinguish recodes from column renames, and harmonise two different site vocabularies.
</div>

**You need:** the client packages and the Opal login from
[Getting started](book-00-start.html). This chapter uses `omop.questionnaire_vocab`,
then the two questionnaire sites.

## Start this chapter

Log in first:

```{r book-local-server, include = FALSE}
# Outputs come from a local DataSHIELD server holding the same resources as the Opal in the text.
library(dsOMOPClient)
source("tools/book/display.R")
source("tools/book/connection.R")
```

```{r chapter-setup, eval = FALSE}
library(dsOMOPClient)
conns <- ds.omop.login("https://localhost:8443", user = "administrator",
  password = "password", resource = "omop.questionnaire_vocab", server = "site")$conns
```

```{r chapter-setup-local-2, include = FALSE}
conns <- book_connect("questionnaire", categories = TRUE)$conns
```

The connection is ready; `conns` identifies the server used by the analysis calls.

[Chapter 4](book-04-vocabulary.html) identifies Education question (**900001**),
Fever question (**900002**), Secondary education (**900101**), University
education (**900102**), Yes (**900301**) and No (**900302**). The later discovery
table in this chapter adds the concepts needed for ordering and classification.

## Translation follows the value type

A concept-valued answer is a category, not a quantity to average. With the recipe API,
the declared value column supplies that type. Your variable name does not need
the suffix `_concept_id`.

```{r category-variable}
education <- omop_variable(
  event = omop_event("observation", concepts = 900001L),
  reducer = omop_reduce("last_value", value = "value_as_concept_id"))
```

The event selects the education question; the reducer reads its last coded answer.
Selecting the question concept as the value would answer a different question.

```{r default-translation}
categories <- omop_recipe(variables = list(education = education),
  outputs = omop_output("people", format = "wide", grain = "person"))
recipe_execute(categories, out = c(people = "D_categories"), conns = conns)
```

Translation is the default. The data values become labelled factors on the server.

```{r default-table}
dsBaseClient::ds.table("D_categories$education",
  useNA = "always", datasources = conns)$output.list$TABLES.COMBINED_all.sources_counts
```

The latest education answers are Secondary education for 12 people and
University education for 12, with 24 missing. The concept IDs used in the definition
are vocabulary identifiers; they do not appear as numeric answers in this table.

## Recode answers to yes and no

An explicit label map changes categories by concept identity. It does not search
for text in each site's database.

First inspect the default wording:

```{r fever-default}
fever_default <- omop_variable(event = omop_event("observation", concepts = 900002L),
  reducer = omop_reduce("last_value", value = "value_as_concept_id"))
fever_recipe <- omop_recipe(variables = list(fever = fever_default))
recipe_execute(fever_recipe, out = "D_fever_default", conns = conns)
dsBaseClient::ds.table("D_fever_default$fever",
  useNA = "always", datasources = conns)$output.list$TABLES.COMBINED_all.sources_counts
```

The default labels are No and Yes, with 12 people each and 24 missing. Now give
those same concepts the study's lower-case wording:

```{r recode}
fever <- omop_variable(
  event = omop_event("observation", concepts = 900002L),
  reducer = omop_reduce("last_value", value = "value_as_concept_id"),
  labels = c("900301" = "yes", "900302" = "no"))
```

Only the declared answer concepts receive those labels. A missing answer stays
missing; absence of a record is not a clinical “no”.

```{r recode-run}
answers <- omop_recipe(variables = list(fever = fever),
  outputs = omop_output("people", format = "wide", grain = "person"))
recipe_execute(answers, out = c(people = "D_category_answers"), conns = conns)
```

```{r recode-table}
recode_table_result <- dsBaseClient::ds.table("D_category_answers$fever", useNA = "no", datasources = conns)
recode_table_result$output.list$TABLES.COMBINED_all.sources_counts
```

There are still 12 “no” and 12 “yes” answers. Only the wording changed; this
call excludes missing answers. `ds.omop.table()` also shows declared zero levels.

## Rename the column separately

A column name identifies the variable; a label names one of its categories.
Name an explicit variable in the recipe list, without changing what its answers mean.

```{r column-rename}
renamed <- omop_recipe(variables = list(schooling = education),
  outputs = omop_output("people", format = "wide", grain = "person"))
recipe_execute(renamed, out = c(people = "D_renamed_categories"), conns = conns)
dsBaseClient::ds.colnames("D_renamed_categories", datasources = conns)
```

The list name supplies the scalar variable name. A concept label map would instead
change values inside that column. The visit walkthrough uses a value-preserving
mutate alias when adding a new name to an already assigned date column.

```{r renamed-table}
ds.omop.table("D_renamed_categories", "schooling", conns = conns)
```

The education categories and counts are unchanged. Next we address differences
between sites, where even the vocabulary names can vary.

## Rename automatically generated concept columns

Some layouts expand one event into separate columns for each concept. A panel
of inpatient/outpatient counts is one example. `column_names` targets their
stable generated keys, not the explicit scalar variable from the previous step.

Inpatient Visit is **9201** and Outpatient Visit is **9202**, found in chapter 4.
First build the unrenamed panel and read its public codebook:

```{r generated-keys}
visit_event <- omop_event("visit_occurrence", c(9201L, 9202L), name = "visits")
visit_population <- omop_population("visitors", index = omop_index(visit_event))
panel_output <- omop_output("panel", rows = "visitors", format = "person_period",
  options = list(sources = list(visit_event), bins = list(width = 30L),
    window = omop_window(0, 29), analyses = "count"))
panel_recipe <- omop_recipe(events = list(visit_event),
  populations = list(visit_population), outputs = panel_output)
panel_result <- recipe_execute(panel_recipe, out = c(panel = "D_original_panel"), conns = conns)
panel_columns <- recipe_codebook(panel_result)[[1]]$columns
panel_columns[c("stable_key", "name")]
```

The count columns have stable keys `visits_9201_count` and `visits_9202_count`.
Use those keys in the rename map:

```{r generated-rename}
renamed_panel <- omop_recipe(events = list(visit_event),
  populations = list(visit_population), outputs = panel_output,
  column_names = c(visits_9201_count = "admissions", visits_9202_count = "outpatient"))
```

The keys combine the source name, concept identity and analysis. They remain
stable when a vocabulary changes its wording; your chosen column names remain
readable. An explicit name cannot collide with a protected or another final column.

```{r generated-run}
recipe_execute(renamed_panel, out = c(panel = "D_renamed_panel"), conns = conns)
dsBaseClient::ds.colnames("D_renamed_panel", datasources = conns)
```

The resulting count columns have your declared names. Using `column_names` with
an explicit scalar name such as `education` would not rename that scalar column.

## Find and save the category identities

For the next examples, `omop.questionnaire_vocab` includes a small synthetic
classification catalogue. Its teaching ATC and ICD-10 entries illustrate the
hierarchy structure; they are not clinical mappings to reuse in a study.
Search the catalogue before choosing an order or a rollup.

```{r category-discovery, table.row.names = FALSE}
catalogue <- ds.omop.concept.search(NULL, standard_only = FALSE, limit = 100,
  conns = conns)$pooled
classification <- catalogue[catalogue$vocabulary_id %in% c("ATC", "ICD10", "Language") |
  catalogue$concept_id %in% c(900101L, 900102L, 900103L, 900201L, 900202L, 950001L), ]
classification[c("concept_id", "concept_name", "vocabulary_id", "concept_class_id")]
ids <- setNames(catalogue$concept_id, catalogue$concept_name)
education_ids <- ids[c("Primary education", "Secondary education", "University education")]
education_keys <- paste0("concept:", education_ids)
education_set <- omop_concept_set(unname(education_ids))
category_file <- "category-concepts.rds"
saveRDS(list(answers = education_set), category_file)
```

The `concept_id` column supplies the IDs we select below, including Primary
education (900103) and English language (4180186). The question and answer concepts were located in observation in
[chapter 4](book-04-vocabulary.html#find-the-questionnaire-questions-answers-and-causes).
Primary education is declared in this catalogue but is not an observed answer.
A complete public category domain can include an unused level.

## Put primary, secondary and tertiary in order

Alphabetical order is not educational order. Declare stable concept identities,
then give the three levels the short labels used by the study.

```{r category-order}
education_ordered <- omop_category(education, omop_category_spec(
  levels = education_keys, order = omop_category_order(education_keys), ordered = TRUE))
ordered_recipe <- omop_recipe(variables = list(education = education_ordered),
  labels = setNames(c("primary", "secondary", "tertiary"), education_ids))
ordered_result <- recipe_execute(ordered_recipe, out = "D_ordered", conns = conns)
ds.omop.table("D_ordered", "education", conns = conns)
```

The table follows primary < secondary < tertiary, with primary = 0, secondary = 12 and tertiary = 12. The 24 missing
answers are omitted by `ds.omop.table()`. The order is chosen by the study, not inferred from frequency.
Ordered factors use polynomial contrasts; set `ordered = FALSE` if your model
needs nominal treatment contrasts instead. A treatment reference and display
order are separate choices.

Check the actual type of this column.

```{r category-order-type}
dsBaseClient::ds.class("D_ordered$education", datasources = conns)
```

It is an ordered factor, not the integers used internally to store a factor.

## Choose labels and language

`name` displays the concept name. `code_name` adds its vocabulary code; it does
not expose numeric concept IDs as answers. A language declaration requests the
vocabulary's synonyms for that language, falling back to a concept name when
none is available. It does not translate free text automatically.

```{r category-language}
language_id <- ids[["English language"]]
styled <- omop_recipe(variables = list(
  code_and_name = omop_category(education, omop_category_spec(label_style = "code_name")),
  local_name = omop_category(education, omop_category_spec(
    language = omop_category_language(language_id)))))
style_result <- recipe_execute(styled, out = "D_styled", conns = conns)
ds.omop.table("D_styled", "local_name", conns = conns)
```

The teaching vocabulary supplies “Secondary schooling” and “Tertiary schooling”
as English synonyms, still with 12 people each. The category identity is unchanged by these labels.

Display the code-and-name variant from the same extraction.

```{r category-style-table}
ds.omop.table("D_styled", "code_and_name", conns = conns)
```

The counts remain 12/12; each label now includes its public vocabulary code.
Use `code` or `vocabulary_code` when that coding context is needed.
`source_code` is refused on this route because source-code disclosure needs a
separately admitted policy. Prefer names for an ordinary analysis table.

## Keep Missing and Unknown distinct

Missing means there is no usable value; Unknown represents OMOP concept zero,
“No matching concept”. It does not mean a respondent answered “Don’t know”.
Neither means a recorded “no”. The missing policy can make each an explicit
factor level without inventing a clinical answer.

```{r category-missing}
missing_recipe <- omop_recipe(variables = list(
  education = omop_category(education, omop_category_spec(
    missing = omop_category_missing(na = "level", unknown = "level"))),
  sex = omop_category(omop_variable(name = "sex", table = "person", column = "gender_concept_id"), omop_category_spec(
    missing = omop_category_missing(na = "level", unknown = "level")))))
missing_result <- recipe_execute(missing_recipe, out = "D_missing_categories", conns = conns)
ds.omop.table("D_missing_categories", "education", conns = conns)
```

The 24 missing answers are now visible as Missing, alongside 12 Secondary and
12 University answers. The public Unknown level may have
zero count; declaring it does not assert that anyone has an unknown answer.
The sex column contains FEMALE and MALE; its declared Missing and Unknown levels
remain empty.

Check which declared sex levels are actually observed.

```{r category-unknown}
ds.omop.table("D_missing_categories", "sex", conns = conns)
```

There are 20 FEMALE and 28 MALE values. A declared Unknown level
does not turn an observed FEMALE or MALE value into an unknown value.

## Present a semantic flag as an integer, logical or factor

A binary reducer asks whether an event occurred. It has false/true meaning before
presentation, so it can safely become 0/1, FALSE/TRUE or named categories.

```{r category-binary}
has_visit <- omop_variable(event = visit_event, reducer = omop_reduce("binary"))
flags <- omop_recipe(variables = list(
  integer_flag = has_visit,
  logical_flag = omop_category(has_visit, omop_category_spec(binary = omop_category_binary("logical"))),
  named_flag = omop_category(has_visit, omop_category_spec(binary = omop_category_binary("factor")))))
flag_result <- recipe_execute(flags, out = "D_flags", conns = conns)
ds.omop.table("D_flags", "named_flag", conns = conns)
```

There are 12 “no” and 36 “yes” values. This “no” means no selected visit in the
defined history. It does not mean a
negative answer to the fever question. Binary presentation is for semantic flags,
not an arbitrary two-level concept column.

Inspect the published types without reading patient rows.

```{r category-binary-types}
flag_columns <- recipe_codebook(flag_result)[[1]]$columns
flag_columns[c("name", "r_type")]
```

The three columns represent the same flag as integer, logical and factor values.
Their R types tell you which representation a downstream method receives.

## Roll up drug classes and cause chapters

A rollup follows vocabulary relationships. ATC levels are classification classes,
not a number of ancestor steps. ICD-10 chapters require the source coding
relationships; a similar-looking code prefix is insufficient.

```{r category-hierarchy-discovery}
drug_id <- ids[["Questionnaire study medicine"]]
ancestors <- ds.omop.concept.ancestors(drug_id, conns = conns)$pooled
ancestors[c("concept_id", "concept_name", "min_levels_of_separation")]
```

The medicine has two teaching ATC ancestors: 960001 and 960002. The separation
counts describe the links; ATC level itself comes from the vocabulary class.

```{r cause-links}
icd_id <- ids[["Teaching circulatory code"]]
links <- rbind(
  ds.omop.concept.relationships(icd_id, relationship_id = "Maps to", conns = conns)$pooled,
  ds.omop.concept.relationships(icd_id, relationship_id = "Is a", conns = conns)$pooled)
links[c("concept_id", "relationship_id", "related_concept_id", "related_concept_name")]
```

Teaching circulatory code (960010) maps to Myocardial infarction (900201)
and belongs to Teaching circulatory chapter (960020). These are synthetic
relationships used only for this lesson.

```{r rollup-occurrence}
ds.omop.concept.locate(c(drug_id, ids[["Myocardial infarction"]]),
  scope = "pooled", conns = conns)$pooled
classification_sets <- list(answers = education_set, drug = omop_concept_set(drug_id),
  causes = omop_concept_set(unname(ids[c("Myocardial infarction", "Pneumonia")])))
saveRDS(classification_sets, category_file)
```

The locator shows banded counts: 35 medicine users means 35–39 and 20
myocardial-infarction records means 20–24. Later category tables release exact
counts when permitted; the 24 infarctions are consistent with the band 20.

Now choose the public domain to be classified. Here *domain* means the declared
set of source concepts, not the OMOP domain such as Drug or Condition.
`omop_category_rows()` selects the concept category on each event row;
`omop_category_reduce()` then chooses one category per person using a reducer. Ambiguous targets fail by default;
resolve them in the study protocol with explicit priority or multiple membership.
Do not resolve ambiguity by inspecting patient frequencies.

```{r category-rollups}
drug_value <- omop_category_reduce(
  omop_category_rows(omop_event("drug_exposure", classification_sets$drug), "concept_id"),
  omop_reduce("last_value"))
cause_value <- omop_category_reduce(
  omop_category_rows(omop_event("death"), "concept_id"), omop_reduce("first_value"))
rollups <- omop_recipe(variables = list(
  atc_first = omop_category(drug_value, omop_category_spec(rollup =
    omop_category_rollup("atc", level = 1, domain = classification_sets$drug))),
  atc_second = omop_category(drug_value, omop_category_spec(rollup =
    omop_category_rollup("atc", level = 2, domain = classification_sets$drug))),
  chapter = omop_category(cause_value, omop_category_spec(rollup =
    omop_category_rollup("icd10", level = "chapter", vocabulary = "ICD10",
      domain = classification_sets$causes)))))
rollup_result <- recipe_execute(rollups, out = "D_rollups", conns = conns)
ds.omop.table("D_rollups", "chapter", conns = conns)
```

There are 24 people in the circulatory chapter and 12 in the respiratory
chapter. The 12 people with no cause are omitted from this table. The cause counts are grouped by the synthetic chapter relationships.
The underlying person roster is unchanged. Use a validated clinical vocabulary
for a real cause-of-death classification.

Compare the two ATC levels in the public codebook.

```{r category-atc-codebook}
rollup_columns <- recipe_codebook(rollup_result)[[1]]$columns
data.frame(
  Variable = c("atc_first", "atc_second"),
  Category = vapply(rollup_columns$levels[match(c("atc_first", "atc_second"), rollup_columns$name)],
    function(x) paste(x$labels, collapse = ", "), character(1)))
```

The first and second levels have different labels even though the same drug
records contribute to both. Neither level is selected by counting observed drugs.

## Read the codebook of each table

Keep the execution result: it carries the admitted table metadata. Calling
`recipe_codebook()` on it is local and does not query patient rows. Calling it
on the recipe instead produces a prospective schema, with unresolved fields
marked explicitly.

```{r category-codebook}
book <- recipe_codebook(ordered_result)
book[[1]]$columns[c("stable_key", "name", "semantic_type", "r_type")]
```

The stable key identifies the column before a display rename. Semantic type tells
you what a value means; R type tells you how it is represented. Units, category
levels, ordering, reference, contrasts and derivation belong with the analysis
protocol. None of these fields is a count or a missingness estimate.

## Two sites with different wording and different answers

Site2 has no Secondary education answers: all its respondents have University
answers. Its newer vocabulary calls concept 900102 “University qualification”.
Log in to both sites. In a real federation each site has its own server URL;
here both resources are on our Opal:

```{r two-connections, eval = FALSE}
ds.omop.disconnect(conns = conns)
DSI::datashield.logout(conns)
conns <- ds.omop.login(rep("https://localhost:8443", 2), user = "administrator",
  password = "password",
  server = c("site", "site2"),
  resource = c(site = "omop.questionnaire", site2 = "omop.questionnaire_site2"))$conns
```

```{r two-connections-local-31, include = FALSE}
conns <- book_switch("questionnaire", two_sites = TRUE)$conns
```

Inspect the vocabulary wording before looking at patient counts:

```{r two-vocabularies}
lookup <- ds.omop.concept.lookup(900102L, conns = conns)
do.call(rbind, lapply(names(lookup$per_site), function(site)
  cbind(Site = site, lookup$per_site[[site]][c("concept_id", "concept_name")])))
```

Both names belong to concept 900102. The ID, not the text, establishes the
shared category.

## Let the default reconciliation work

Execute the original unlabelled education recipe on both connections:

```{r two-default}
recipe_execute(categories, out = c(people = "D_default_pair"), conns = conns)
paired_counts <- dsBaseClient::ds.table("D_default_pair$education",
  useNA = "always", datasources = conns)
paired_counts$output.list$TABLES.COMBINED_all.sources_counts
```

The pooled counts are Secondary education = 12, University qualification = 36,
and missing = 48. Execution already chooses one category per concept ID and
uses the newest vocabulary wording. No label map was needed to combine them.

The same two-site response contains counts for each site. Select the count
matrix, leaving out the proportion matrices:

```{r site-counts}
site_counts <- paired_counts$output.list$TABLE_rvar.by.study_counts
colnames(site_counts) <- names(conns)
site_counts
```

Site has 12 Secondary and 12 University qualification answers, plus 24 missing.
Site2 has Secondary = 0, University qualification = 24 and missing = 24.
The joint response includes the zero cell and shared wording. Adding its two
columns gives the pooled counts above. These are counts from separate sites;
this operation does not link people across sites.

## Exercise 1: fix the study's wording

Keep “University education” as the shared label even when a site has a newer
name. Does this change the counts?

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```{r shared-label}
harmonised <- omop_recipe(variables = list(education = education),
  labels = c("900102" = "University education"),
  outputs = omop_output("people", format = "wide", grain = "person"))
recipe_execute(harmonised, out = c(people = "D_shared"), conns = conns)
dsBaseClient::ds.table("D_shared$education",
  useNA = "always", datasources = conns)$output.list$TABLES.COMBINED_all.sources_counts
```

The counts remain 12 Secondary, 36 University and 48 missing. The explicit map
fixes wording; it changes neither the question selected nor the answer IDs.

</details>

## Exercise 2: combine two answer categories deliberately

Give Secondary and University the same label, “Recorded education”. Predict the
pooled count and missing count before executing.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```{r merge-labels}
merged <- omop_recipe(variables = list(education = education),
  labels = c("900101" = "Recorded education", "900102" = "Recorded education"))
recipe_execute(merged, out = "D_merged_answers", conns = conns)
dsBaseClient::ds.table("D_merged_answers$education",
  useNA = "always", datasources = conns)$output.list$TABLES.COMBINED_all.sources_counts
```

Equal labels merge the two categories: 48 people have Recorded education and
48 remain missing across the two sites. This discards the education-level
distinction; shared wording alone cannot establish clinical equivalence.

</details>

## Exercise 3: change the reducer at both sites

Take the first education answer instead of the last. Does either site's
marginal distribution change?

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```{r first-at-sites}
first_answers <- omop_recipe(variables = list(education = omop_variable(
  event = omop_event("observation", concepts = 900001L),
  reducer = omop_reduce("first_value", value = "value_as_concept_id"))))
recipe_execute(first_answers, out = "D_first_pair", conns = conns)
first_counts <- dsBaseClient::ds.table("D_first_pair$education",
  useNA = "always", datasources = conns)
first_site_counts <- first_counts$output.list$TABLE_rvar.by.study_counts
colnames(first_site_counts) <- names(conns)
first_site_counts
```

Site remains 12 Secondary, 12 University qualification and 24 missing; site2
remains 0, 24 and 24. Adding the columns again gives pooled totals 12, 36 and 48. At site, opposite individual changes cancel in the margins, as
chapter 5 showed. At site2 every recorded answer is University. Equal totals
do not prove that the first and last selections identify the same answers.

</details>
## Recap

Concept values translate by default. Label maps recode answers; column maps rename variables. Execution reconciles categories by concept ID; explicit labels fix study wording.
A missing category at one site remains an explicit zero after reconciliation.

**Next:** [11. Reusable studies and standalone verbs](book-11-parameters.html).
